      "content": "Yes, rapid pH drops are indeed often caused by lactobacillus accelerating due to higher temperatures, but it isn't necessarily \"hijacked\" if the yeast population remains vigorous enough to produce carbon dioxide alongside the acid. You can monitor this by tracking the pH of each feed—starting paper testing when pH drops below 4.0 or using a digital meter—and watching for a steep decline that doesn't recover within four hours, which signals the risk of over-fermentation. Most importantly, fixing the room temperature often allows the natural balance to self-correct without needing extreme interventions.",
